  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $91%$ of those infected and $4%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $15%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is not infected? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|T)=\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P(T|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $91%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.91,\\,P(T|{D}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $15%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.15,\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.15=0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|T) & =\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P(T|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.85)}{(0.04)(0.85)+(0.91)(0.15)} \\\\  & ≈20%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent.",
